<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pa'O people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pa-O, Taungthu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pa'O are a Karen-speaking people of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> of Myanmar, living mainly around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunggyi">Taunggyi</a> and the hills near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inle-lake">Inle Lake</a>, and are one of the country's larger ethnic minorities. Traditionally Buddhist farmers known for growing thanaphet leaves for cheroots, they lived under Shan princes such as those of Yawnghwe and Mongnai. Since independence Pa'O groups have had their own armed organisations and a self-administered zone.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pao-people.jpg" alt="Pa'O people" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: exfordy · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pa-O_women_selling_vegetables,_Myanmar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Pa'O are linked in their history with the Thaton region of lower Myanmar. They once wore colourful clothing, but after King Anawratha defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuha">Manuha</a>, the Mon king of Thaton, the Pa'O were enslaved and made to wear <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>-dyed clothes as a sign of their status. Their origin myth holds that they descend from a shaman, or weizza, and a female dragon, and the Pa'O flag badge that men and women pin to their jackets represents the two. They are also known as the Black Karen, Pa-U and Taungthu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Pa'O are the second largest ethnic group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> and also live in Kayin, Kayah and Mon states and the Bago Division; many have fled conflict to Mae Hong Son Province in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. They fall into the Lowland Pa'O of Thaton and the Highland Pa'O of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunggyi">Taunggyi</a>, with as many as twenty-four subgroups. Besides thanapet leaves they grow onions, garlic, chili, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a>, beans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesame">sesame</a> and green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>.</p><p>Most Pa'O follow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and some are Christian or hold animist beliefs. Boys are initiated as novice monks at Poy Sang Long, and in Buddhist Lent young people carry lanterns three times round the temple. From April to July the Fire Rocket Festival brings villages together for a week to fire rockets, once of bamboo and now of iron, to help the clouds make rain. Pa'O National Day falls on the full moon of Tabaung in March and honours ancestors and leaders such as King Suriya Janthar.</p><p>Highland men traditionally wear a turban, a white shirt, a dark jacket and long black trousers, and women a five-piece outfit with a turban and two large conical hairpins. At weddings guests tie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> threads round the wrists of the couple. Common people are buried and monks cremated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pa'O people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its saopha ruled rice valleys and hill country populated by Shan, <b>Pa-O</b> and other peoples, paying tribute to Burmese kings in Ava and later Mandalay, and sending daughters to the royal court.</blockquote><a href="/state/mongnai-state">Mongnai State</a></li><li><blockquote>Its saophas ruled a lake and valley economy of floating gardens, rice and fishing worked by Intha, Shan, <b>Pa-O</b> and Danu peoples.</blockquote><a href="/state/yawnghwe-state">Yawnghwe State</a></li></ul></section>
